Phoenix RPi4: upload the screen-pixmap Y-flip in ONE call, not one call per row.

xorg-server-21.1.24-glamor-screen-upload-yflip.patch made CPU uploads to the X
screen pixmap Y-mirrored, to match the raster-flipped rendered content. It did the
flip the simplest way: one glTexSubImage2D per row.

That is correct, and pathologically slow in the configuration the desktop actually
runs in. `startx_gpu` uses Xphoenix-glamor-daemon, which reaches the GPU through
/dev/v3d-srv -- so every GL call is an RPC to another process. A 640x480 XPutImage
from the GL demo client therefore cost 480 round trips per frame, and that window
was measured updating about once a second while full-screen in-process GL runs at
35 fps.

This flips into a packed scratch buffer and issues a single bulk upload: same
destination rows, same bytes, one RPC instead of N. The memcpy is cached-to-cached
and far cheaper than the calls it replaces. Out of memory falls back to the
original per-row loop, which is still correct.

Destination arithmetic, since it is easy to get wrong: the row loop sent source
row `yy` to texel row `(h-1-yy) - box->y1`, so over yy in [y1,y2) the texel rows
span `(h-y2) - box->y1` upward. The bulk call therefore uses origin
`(h-y2) - box->y1` with height `rows`, and scratch row 0 must be source row y2-1.
GL_UNPACK_ROW_LENGTH is set to 0 around the call because the scratch is packed at
the box width rather than at the source stride, and restored afterwards.

Applies on top of the -yflip patch.

--- a/glamor/glamor_transfer.c
